            {
               "text": "### Everything Together\n\nThe picture above shows the history of the universe according to the Lambda-CDM model. After Cosmic Inflation, the universe began to form the particles we are about to study (<htcite0>).\n\n#### Who came first?\n\nRight after Inflation, the first particles began to appear in the universe. To understand them, let’s reflect:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
                     "text": "©ESA – C. Carreau",
                     "page": "History of Cosmic Formation",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: The universe is like me</b><br /><br />The laws of the universe do not change over time. So we can look at our own growth and compare it with the development of the universe.<br /><br />We start with the union of our parents’ cells and grow inside our mother until we are born. After the baby stage, we become children; then adolescents; and finally, adults.<br /><br />Would it make sense for the universe to start with large structures already in place, like the Moon, stars, and planets?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Just as we started as tiny cells and grew into who we are, the universe also started small.\n\n#### First Particles\n\nRight after the end of Inflation — which lasted less than a second — the first elementary particles began to appear (<htcite0>).\n\nParticles have existed in the universe for billions of years, but many elementary particles were only discovered and understood in the 20th century, according to the Gregorian calendar.\n\nThe idea of something indivisible, called the “atom”, originated with Democritus, around <htdate0> (<htcite1>).\n\nAs knowledge advanced and machines allowed more precise measurements, we learned that atomic models like Rutherford’s (<htcite2>; <htcite3>), shown in Graph 1, considered only the electron as a fundamental particle (<htcite4>).\n\nProtons and neutrons, for example, are made of even smaller particles, which we now call quarks (<htcite5>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
                     "text": "©ESA – C. Carreau",
                     "page": "History of Cosmic Formation",
                     "date_time": {
